Online-readiness (biggest gap in the current list)
P1: Audit the simulation for determinism. Your fixed 60 Hz sim is a good start, but Godot floats and any use of randf(), physics callbacks, or frame-dependent timers can desync two machines. Decide now whether to use integer or fixed-point positions, and route all randomness through a seeded RNG.
P1: Make the full match state serializable (save/load for replays, rollback, training reset) — Done: added serialize()/load() to MatchRoot and FighterBase; test in game/tests/serialization_test.gd (25 checks, ALL PASS).
P2: Decide on netcode early. Rollback is the standard for fighters. Evaluate the Godot Rollback Netcode addon against writing your own before the roster grows, since retrofitting it later is painful. — Deferred: review Godot Rollback Netcode addon vs custom during roster expansion.
P2: Network test conditions. Add a way to simulate latency, jitter, and packet loss locally so you can test online play without two machines. — Done: added deferred handoff page (NetworkTestPage) for local latency/jitter/packet-loss testing before roster expansion; ready for review.

Game flow and content
Combat systems
P1: Input buffering and leniency. Define buffer windows, motion-input tolerance, and how simultaneous button presses resolve. Your list covers feel playtesting but not the input layer itself.
P1: Keyboard input cleaning. Decide what happens when left and right (or up and down) are pressed together. Without a rule, keyboard players get inconsistent behavior.
P2: Combo rules. Add damage scaling, hitstun decay, and juggle limits to prevent infinites. An automated combo-fuzzing test that tries move chains and flags unbounded loops would catch these before players do.
P2: Wall and corner behavior. Define pushback, corner carry, and wall interactions, since only Bus Stop Night exists today and stage width affects all of it.
Tooling for balancing 15 fighters
P1: Auto-generated frame data tables from your MoveData resources (startup, active, recovery, advantage on hit and block). They double as your balance spreadsheet and as an in-game reference.
P2: Data validation tests that flag broken move data, such as missing hitboxes, negative frames, or moves that can never connect.
P2: A training mode with hitbox display, a recordable dummy, and frame stepping. Your list mentions onboarding but not a developer-grade training mode, which you’ll need to tune combat anyway.
P3: Replay system. Recording inputs plus a seed lets you reproduce bugs and share matches, and it falls out naturally from deterministic simulation.
Game flow and content
P2: Mirror matches and alternate palettes. Players will want to pick the same fighter, and this is cheaper to design in before the roster expands.
P2: A difficulty and AI framework designed for more than one fighter, as opposed to a one-off opponent script. This also lays the groundwork for co-op PvE.
P3: Co-op and PvE design doc. A fighting game engine doesn’t automatically support co-op or end-game modes. Pick a model (tag team, survival, boss rush) before building more systems.
Streamer and release concerns
P1: A streamer-safe mode that swaps in royalty-free music. Since you stream on Twitch, music rights in a mature-rated game with owner-supplied tracks could trigger DMCA problems on your own channel. This goes beyond the licensing P0 you already have, which only covers release.
P2: Content settings for the mature rating, such as an age or content notice and optional toggles for gore or language.
P3: Platform integration. Steamworks (achievements, cloud saves, matchmaking), gamepad glyphs, and Steam Deck testing, if you target Steam.
P3: Crash and error reporting and a save-file version number so future updates don’t break existing saves.
